(111356, 'wiver', 'Wiver', 'wiver', '<i>n.</i>', 'Wiv\"er', '(?)', '  [OE.\r <i>wivere</i> a serpent, OF. <i>wivre</i>, <i>guivre</i>, F. <i>givre</i>,\r <i>guivre</i>, wiver, from L. <i>vipera</i>; probably influenced by OHG.\r <i>wipera</i>, from the Latin.  See <u>Viper</u>, and cf.\r <u>Weever</u>.]</p>\r \r <p>', 1660, 1),
